#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124304 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "4c1e01c1-d607-5ad8-8643-b3317201ab9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4832" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{c}_1=\\mathbf{b}_2-4\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2=5\\mathbf{b}_1+7\\mathbf{b}_2,$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](images/e6/e62970e0d84f224be492efe709d4df42872cbcc9e68c136e2f73b0e36e18f5c4.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/5f/5f5ad323ae8875717d552f068c827295954ecdb2c802f5fb3899c18884aa1f1f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124305 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "93b466b2-9b8d-55fa-87b8-b5bf24a497cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4833" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](images/82/827f1a79a51ea88e59f5d766ee531cacf3945cc2ce649521c7b1d8e7eea35e06.png)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](images/82/82d0eab6a51dbf1615a3e2bb4fc6ffef622a38ac3c980dc742b552946c11a87a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](images/0d/0d9a23830f48d4659dd963110d9322c12e8d4849bd9bcd63ae9d5adb0e1fba05.png)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](images/10/10b5f2e35da56c7be6d547827164c68f475df4d012116b9666c12a94bbc39f55.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/06/06ef110e61fcdf044e15300d50bc5ac918b37e8351c1aa6b77124dd2029c9aa3.png)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124306 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6c5b5534-2082-593e-af07-df83497ea84c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4835" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](images/07/07a8e394234737d51a0064816b255569d20e826eb3a82884a24f1fc0a9fa6baa.png)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "0929855b-0310-5c87-8cff-cc2faa8a5ae0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4837" :question/problem "Let ![](images/51/51b47f81b941fffa62d1f8402a4ef08b9b7d1636b1a37a86578311f1db192b3d.png) and ![](images/a9/a9964c0672c8859f4b4857d422e25a435ed0be907c074a22b7cc12729f051613.png) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/60/60eb4bbd057feab1b78fb283e951dc8c35f9a95875bd46f078c87c25b3b749c8.png)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c074f1f1-190d-5c2b-be44-47f1519f9e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386642} {:answer/id #uuid "428736dc-252f-56df-b2cf-058df8e2f2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386643} {:answer/id #uuid "4293ab9b-7451-59be-b692-d3049e359a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386644} {:answer/id #uuid "a593476a-084b-51fd-b813-f788df67a1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386645} {:answer/id #uuid "dba8598e-a9a8-5938-a8b8-24e380b6f380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a593476a-084b-51fd-b813-f788df67a1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386645} :answer-field/id #uuid "570439f3-213e-5838-a79e-eae761e0d96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bcd060e-67aa-5a69-a9d1-f310cdace57a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113105" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=2{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "919eb1e9-2f4b-56b6-88e5-3859874737bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386677} {:answer/id #uuid "90eeb8b6-e3b5-53db-a5fe-f9746d4ccb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386678} {:answer/id #uuid "b872cd7b-0659-5c54-8111-e27c28adc0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386679} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdc24c8-890c-5fc5-a78e-92d48b19a269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386680} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bec6ef-0b83-555d-8bfe-9ff77d315986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "919eb1e9-2f4b-56b6-88e5-3859874737bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386677} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7670fd-c5bc-51f7-98ba-bd3ac9cdc072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e492abb-150e-5b29-ab40-6db339f73d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113415" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ed2a8aa-4268-52a7-9ba6-22a2ed6e225a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122097} {:answer/id #uuid "1187199c-956c-56c5-a525-22614015a188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122098} {:answer/id #uuid "20cebc03-2cda-5e1b-878b-b878ae1fc725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122099} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee8edd9-3e22-55d1-814a-fd6c6a4ccc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122100} {:answer/id #uuid "3c577af1-710b-51ac-a28b-7df97ef3cb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122101}] :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa9fb36-2d84-563f-84b3-a00ac62830b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122096}] :question/id #uuid "f27d85cd-7d1d-52da-929b-4fa8b04cc954" :question/math-academy-id "q-35805" :question/problem "Let $B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of $R^{2}$. Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "413babe6-bd06-53d8-b116-6f3c125d0b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321665} {:answer/id #uuid "437a9aba-29f6-58b7-9e94-335db5d38686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321680} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbeaebe-5b9a-59b4-b02f-a834002d78a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321873} {:answer/id #uuid "5da43626-5807-5c19-9ce4-b9a4f29b4e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321886} {:answer/id #uuid "97341cc3-b696-5ed4-bcde-4f652a3e90a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5da43626-5807-5c19-9ce4-b9a4f29b4e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321886} :answer-field/id #uuid "a89f17c1-0ec4-5bf7-9f2a-f1f548e39054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7561091-5a13-5f20-989c-89982f4b4b54" :question/math-academy-id "q-36366" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520cf59b-0e8a-5135-a5bb-58dbfaac9d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349192} {:answer/id #uuid "63a6a38a-272b-5ef8-ac5d-9b8a62db6e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349359} {:answer/id #uuid "a929d2cb-f110-55b5-829c-d6e3d6ec6f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349954} {:answer/id #uuid "b73754bf-f54b-5788-b870-664463b6849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350069} {:answer/id #uuid "efee334f-bad5-5592-a240-43676034e441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efee334f-bad5-5592-a240-43676034e441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8f9c2f-e58d-54c9-bff6-90fe41f9bb30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ff70514-6088-5578-a352-2d83d0571687" :question/math-academy-id "q-81394" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =5{c}_{3}+2{c}_{2}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={c}_{2}-{c}_{1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ a & b & 1 \\\\ -1 & c & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41e4b858-0939-54cf-9dda-08263bf19bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349044} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4ebaf4-f6ae-5d83-b8f7-d86c8df809a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=0,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349136} {:answer/id #uuid "677d8855-5a11-57ef-aca3-3b105f83a4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349398} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffbd4c9-7f71-5f70-aa18-f035562c2e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349475} {:answer/id #uuid "8948bc51-f3d5-5f6c-9eff-8fbe6334f755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=0,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41e4b858-0939-54cf-9dda-08263bf19bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349044} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf2b4e0-ef90-544d-9360-0816914a3219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25384ce4-a891-57c8-b633-4441e98187a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81825" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =3{b}_{3}-{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4{b}_{2}-5{b}_{1}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $C$ relative to the basis $B$ are\n\n$$\n[{c}_{1}]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[{c}_{2}]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[{c}_{3}]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4d259b5-915f-51f3-b82a-a4a9da133e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377846} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4211cf-c344-5620-b95d-15c507cdd614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377847} {:answer/id #uuid "f98495bd-241d-5544-a4a3-84ecc92ef750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377848} {:answer/id #uuid "81b2d894-9407-5098-bffb-afc7c02dc3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377849} {:answer/id #uuid "af02a450-6787-5267-a45c-7a4ff98dfd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4d259b5-915f-51f3-b82a-a4a9da133e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377846} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78e9f40-0718-5bad-956f-0b197faf152e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43d15cca-f579-50c3-b1dc-880062749688" :question/math-academy-id "q-81850" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & a \\\\ b & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & c & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01517329-3e28-5805-9aac-584f93b1c3a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348463} {:answer/id #uuid "7366ef02-a1d3-52aa-8a52-8f56ad1cbc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349508} {:answer/id #uuid "7953ac21-87d0-5f72-9103-11cc5e921515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349566} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d93798-cb62-5237-98c5-12c645b92461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350337} {:answer/id #uuid "e43d5839-5161-5f91-9c65-2cdc6114d39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7366ef02-a1d3-52aa-8a52-8f56ad1cbc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349508} :answer-field/id #uuid "6039748f-1eb1-57b4-8f7e-0fdd49400251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf13df2-1351-5dd8-a600-8ae95cd08849" :question/math-academy-id "q-81855" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-5)-(-7)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070ecd32-2b28-56a5-85bd-6a34995dde85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377853} {:answer/id #uuid "d38da1ce-9f22-5dc7-9d87-db349fd71d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377854} {:answer/id #uuid "0b419e01-bc76-5a21-9ceb-6f1d6f2a07cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377855} {:answer/id #uuid "9298715d-b632-5fb7-a00c-0b61f088dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377856} {:answer/id #uuid "82445d41-0f67-53ce-9020-32e4dd244a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 & -15 \\\\ -15 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9298715d-b632-5fb7-a00c-0b61f088dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377856} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9e6ef9-e9ea-5c1f-ad73-6228ba8a6770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89bdb866-da97-5894-836a-c2ba9ba48e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-81910" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
